      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to find the prime factorization of 400. The prime factorization of 400 is:\n\\[400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for finding the sum of the divisors of a number. If a number \\( n \\) has the prime factorization \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\), then the sum of the divisors of \\( n \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\n\nFor \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) = (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) = 31 \\times 31 = 961 \\]\n\nNow we need to determine the distinct prime factors of 961. We have:\n\\[ 961 = 31^2 \\]\n\nThus, the distinct prime factor of 961 is 31. The number of distinct prime factors of 961 is 1. The final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]\n\nHere is the Python code to verify this solution:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Prime factorization of 400\nprime_factors_400 = sp.factorint(400)\nprime_factors = list(prime_factors_400.keys())\n\n# Sum of divisors of 400\nsum_divisors = sum(sp.mobius(i) * primorial(prime_factors_400[i] + 1) for i in prime_factors)\n\n# Find distinct prime factors of the sum of divisors\ndistinct_prime_factors = set(sp.primefactors(sum_divisors))\n\n# Number of distinct prime factors\nnum_distinct_prime_factors = len(distinct_prime_factors)\nprint(num_distinct_prime_factors)\n```",
