      "text": "To determine the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of 400, we first need to find the prime factorization of 400. We have:\n\n\\[ 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2. \\]\n\nThe sum of the divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}). \\]\n\nFor \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2). \\]\n\nNow we compute each sum separately:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31, \\]\n\\[ 1 + 5 + 25 = 31. \\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 31^2 = 961. \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the prime factorization of 961. We have:\n\n\\[ 961 = 31^2. \\]\n\nThe prime factorization of 961 is simply \\( 31^2 \\), which means 961 has only one distinct prime factor, 31. Therefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of 400 is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{1}. \\]",
